arm64: dts: mediatek: gemini: enable i2c6 for the A72 cluster's supply

MT6797 is tri-cluster and the two Cortex-A72 cores have never been
powered: MP2_CPUSYS_PWR_CON reads its reset value -- unpowered, isolated,
held in reset with its SRAM down -- while both A53 clusters read identical
"on" values (B-40). One of the missing pieces is the cluster's core rail,
VPROC2, and the vendor names its provider precisely: a Dialog DA9214 at
7-bit 0x68 on bus 6 (drivers/misc/mediatek/power/mt6797/da9214.c).

This enables the bus and adds NO child node, which is the point. The
DA9214 is a dual buck; VPROC2 is BUCKB (the vendor's DA9214_VPROC2 is
register 0xD9, and mainline's DA9211_REG_VBUCKB_A is 0xD9), which means
BUCKA is a separate CPU core rail that may well be the one the running
A53 clusters depend on. The regulator core disables regulators nothing
has claimed, so declaring a mis-identified one is not a harmless mistake
on a machine whose only display would go with the CPUs.

With the bus up and CONFIG_REGULATOR_DA9211=m, the chip can be probed
deliberately -- both bucks' enable bits and voltages read after boot,
after the disable-unused sweep has already run -- and the regulator node
lands as a separate change once that measurement exists.

Pins were already defined in mt6797.dtsi (i2c6_pins_a: SDA=GPIO152,
SCL=GPIO151); only the enable was missing.
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
@@ -717,6 +717,43 @@
 	};
 };
 
+/*
+ * i2c6: the DA9214 dual buck that supplies VPROC2 — the Cortex-A72 cluster's
+ * core rail. Enabled with NO child node on purpose.
+ *
+ * WHY THE BUS AND NOT THE REGULATOR
+ *
+ * MT6797 is tri-cluster and cpu8/cpu9 (the two A72s) have never been powered:
+ * MP2_CPUSYS_PWR_CON reads its reset value, unpowered and isolated and held in
+ * reset, while both A53 clusters read identical "on" values (B-40). One of the
+ * things missing is the cluster's supply, and the vendor names it precisely —
+ * `DA9214_SLAVE_ADDR_WRITE 0xD0` (7-bit 0x68) on `da9214_BUSNUM` 6, in
+ * drivers/misc/mediatek/power/mt6797/da9214.c. Mainline's da9211 driver covers
+ * DA9214.
+ *
+ * But the DA9214 is a DUAL buck, and which of BUCKA/BUCKB is VPROC2 is not
+ * established — the other one may well be a rail the running system depends
+ * on. Declaring a regulator we have mis-identified is not a harmless mistake:
+ * the regulator core disables unclaimed regulators at late init, so guessing
+ * wrong could switch off a live supply on a machine whose only display is the
+ * panel it would take with it.
+ *
+ * So this step brings up the bus and nothing else. With i2c6 present, the chip
+ * can be read from userspace with i2cget — presence, both bucks' enable bits
+ * and both voltage registers — and the identification becomes a measurement
+ * instead of an inference. The regulator node lands in a separate change once
+ * that read exists.
+ *
+ * Pins are already defined in mt6797.dtsi (i2c6_pins_a: SDA=GPIO152,
+ * SCL=GPIO151); only the enable was missing.
+ */
+&i2c6 {
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&i2c6_pins_a>;
+	clock-frequency = <400000>;
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
 /* --- Pinctrl --- */
 
 
